ents best explains the law of conservation of mass?</p>\",\"type\":\"text\"}],\"options\":[{\"id\":\"1d58b9fc-6aba-4469-8c52-7b94ecc41b08\",\"key\":\"A\",\"content\":[{\"html\":\"<p>100 g of water is heated to give steam.</p>\",\"type\":\"text\"}],\"rawText\":\"<p>100 g of water is heated to give steam.</p>\",\"isCorrect\":false,\"option_key\":\"a\"},{\"id\":\"852f9f5b-facf-41d9-89e1-912f081a6684\",\"key\":\"B\",\"content\":[{\"html\":\"<p>A sample of N₂ gas is heated at constant pressure without any change in mass.</p>\",\"type\":\"text\"}],\"rawText\":\"<p>A sample of N₂ gas is heated at constant pressure without any change in mass.</p>\",\"isCorrect\":false,\"option_key\":\"b\"},{\"id\":\"55a071fd-9073-4617-bc0a-918116a6d9f6\",\"key\":\"C\",\"content\":[{\"html\":\"<p>36 g of carbon combines with 32 g of oxygen to form 68 g of CO₂.</p>\",\"type\":\"text\"}],\"rawText\":\"<p>36 g of carbon combines with 32 g of oxygen to form 68 g of CO₂.</p>\",\"isCorrect\":true,\"option_key\":\"c\"},{\"id\":\"2b87d16a-a31b-4e1a-86c8-5ef1e957c40d\",\"key\":\"D\",\"content\":[{\"html\":\"<p>10 g of carbon is heated in vacuum without any change in mass.</p>\",\"type\":\"text\"}],\"rawText\":\"<p>10 g of carbon is heated in vacuum without any change in mass.</p>\",\"isCorrect\":false,\"option_key\":\"d\"}],\"rawText\":\"<p>Which of the following statements best explains the law of conservation of mass?</p>\",\"subject\":\"Chemistry\",\"chapterId\":\"b0eebc99-9c0b-4ef8-bb6d-6bb9bd380b13\",\"createdAt\":\"2026-08-31T06:14:29.906+00:00\",\"subjectId\":\"a0eebc99-9c0b-4ef8-bb6d-6bb9bd380a12\",\"updatedAt\":\"2026-08-31T06:14:29.906+00:00\",\"chapter_id\":\"b0eebc99-9c0b-4ef8-bb6d-6bb9bd380b13\",\"difficulty\":\"Easy\",\"subject_id\":\"a0eebc99-9c0b-4ef8-bb6d-6bb9bd380a12\",\"explanation\":[],\"chapter_name\":\"Some Basic Concepts of Chemistry\",\"optionLayout\":\"grid_2x2\",\"questionCode\":\"CHE-SOM-1402\",\"questionType\":\"MCQ_SINGLE\",\"subject_name\":\"Chemistry\",\"correctAnswer\":\"C\",\"correctOption\":\"c\",\"negativeMarks\":1,\"question_code\":\"CHE-SOM-1402\",\"question_type\":\"MCQ_SINGLE\",\"correct_option\":\"c\"},\"questionId\":\"de9e7241-e4e4-4e06-84b3-6c8d20e3bd21\"},{\"id\":\"blk-1788170105297-1-12\",\"type\":\"question\",\"question\":{\"id\":\"78fc5dcc-c0e6-44ad-94dc-c4087c4699fb\",\"year\":null,\"marks\":4,\"source\":\"System Admin\",\"chapter\":\"Some Basic Concepts of Chemistry\",\"content\":[{\"id\":\"blk-1\",\"text\":\"<p>100 g of ethylene polymerises to polythene according to the equation:</p><p>nCH₂ = CH₂ → {CH₂ — CH₂}ₙ</p><p>The mass of polythene produced will be</p>\",\"type\":\"text\"}],\"options\":[{\"id\":\"e75b99d9-ccec-4efc-9f83-e71bb2dd97cb\",\"key\":\"A\",\"content\":[{\"html\":\"<p>100 n g</p>\",\"type\":\"text\"}],\"rawText\":\"<p>100 n g</p>\",\"isCorrect\":false,\"option_key\":\"a\"},{\"id\":\"fd95552e-521d-4b7a-a7b7-a78bbb3f2023\",\"key\":\"B\",\"content\":[{\"html\":\"<p>100/n g</p>\",\"type\":\"text\"}],\"rawText\":\"<p>100/n g</p>\",\"isCorrect\":false,\"option_key\":\"b\"},{\"id\":\"ad2a9664-dd1b-4454-886e-7f6ecffb1f06\",\"key\":\"C\",\"content\":[{\"html\":\"<p>100 n/2 g</p>\",\"type\":\"text\"}],\"rawText\":\"<p>100 n/2 g</p>\",\"isCorrect\":false,\"option_key\":\"c\"},{\"id\":\"7a15bfcc-ff06-4591-8b4c-b8cf891e35cc\",\"key\":\"D\",\"content\":[{\"html\":\"<p>100 g</p>\",\"type\":\"text\"}],\"rawText\":\"<p>100 g</p>\",\"isCorrect\":true,\"option_key\":\"d\"}],\"rawText\":\"<p>100 g of ethylene polymerises to polythene according to the equation:</p><p>nCH₂ = CH₂ → {CH₂ — CH₂}ₙ</p><p>The mass of polythene produced will be</p>\",\"subject\":\"Chemistry\",\"chapterId\":\"b0eebc99-9c0b-4ef8-bb6d-6bb9bd380b13\",\"createdAt\":\"2026-08-31T06:18:08.392+00:00\",\"subjectId\":\"a0eebc99-9c0b-4ef8-bb6d-6bb9bd380a12\",\"updatedAt\":\"2026-08-31T06:18:08.392+00:00\",\"chapter_id\":\"b0eebc99-9c0b-4ef8-bb6d-6bb9bd380b13\",\"difficulty\":\"Easy\",\"subject_id\":\"a0eebc99-9c0b-4ef8-bb6d-6bb9bd380a12\",\"explanation\":[],\"chapter_name\":\"Some Basic Concepts of Chemistry\",\"optionLayout\":\"grid_2x2\",\"questionCode\":\"CHE-SOM-9264\",\"questionType\":\"MCQ_SINGLE\",\"subject_name\":\"Chemistry\",\"correctAnswer\":\"D\",\"correctOption\":\"d\",\"negativeMarks\":1,\"question_code\":\"CHE-SOM-9264\",\"question_type\":\"MCQ_SINGLE\",\"correct_option\":\"d\"},\"questionId\":\"78fc5dcc-c0e6-44ad-94dc-c4087c4699fb\"},{\"id\":\"blk-1788170105297-1-13\",\"type\":\"question\",\"question\":{\"id\":\"9e3730a7-258a-4936-980f-1aa9cfb05f50\",\"year\":null,\"marks\":4,\"source\":\"System Admin\",\"chapter\":\"Some Basic Concepts of Chemistry\",\"content\":[{\"id\":\"blk-1\",\"text\":\"<p>4.88 g of KClO₃ when heated produced 1.92 g of O₂ and 2.96 g of KCl. Which of the following statements regarding the experiment is correct?</p>\",\"type\":\"text\"}],\"options\":[{\"id\":\"6038a087-447f-4952-a061-0de56dddb3f6\",\"key\":\"A\",\"content\":[{\"html\":\"<p>The result illustrates the law of conservation of mass.</p>\",\"type\":\"text\"}],\"rawText\":\"<p>The result illustrates the law of conservation of mass.</p>\",\"isCorrect\":true,\"option_key\":\"a\"},{\"id\":\"d9824704-7546-43e3-889c-0a6253e5a00d\",\"key\":\"B\",\"content\":[{\"html\":\"<p>The result illustrates the law of multiple proportions.</p>\",\"type\":\"text\"}],\"rawText\":\"<p>The result illustrates the law of multiple proportions.</p>\",\"isCorrect\":false,\"option_key\":\"b\"},{\"id\":\"0fa012d4-7859-45bf-a9ae-d2507f5523c1\",\"key\":\"C\",\"content\":[{\"html\":\"<p>The result illustrates the law of constant proportion.</p>\",\"type\":\"text\"}],\"rawText\":\"<p>The result illustrates the law of constant proportion.</p>\",\"isCorrect\":false,\"option_key\":\"c\"},{\"id\":\"7ea9d463-e65b-4824-995e-1dfdd5082145\",\"key\":\"D\",\"content\":[{\"html\":\"<p>None of the above laws is followed.</p>\",\"type\":\"text\"}],\"rawText\":\"<p>None of the above laws is followed.</p>\",\"isCorrect\":false,\"option_key\":\"d\"}],\"rawText\":\"<p>4.88 g of KClO₃ when heated produced 1.92 g of O₂ and 2.96 g of KCl. Which of the following statements regarding the experiment is correct?</p>\",\"subject\":\"Chemistry\",\"chapterId\":\"b0eebc99-9c0b-4ef8-bb6d-6bb9bd380b13\",\"createdAt\":\"2026-08-31T06:12:12.069+00:00\",\"subjectId\":\"a0eebc99-9c0b-4ef8-bb6d-6bb9bd380a12\",\"updatedAt\":\"2026-08-31T08:02:01.023+00:00\",\"chapter_id\":\"b0eebc99-9c0b-4ef8-bb6d-6bb9bd380b13\",\"difficulty\":\"Medium\",\"subject_id\":\"a0eebc99-9c0b-4ef8-bb6d-6bb9bd380a12\",\"explanation\":[],\"chapter_name\":\"Some Basic Concepts of Chemistry\",\"optionLayout\":\"grid_2x2\",\"questionCode\":\"CHE-SOM-4206\",\"questionType\":\"MCQ_SINGLE\",\"subject_name\":\"Chemistry\",\"correctAnswer\":\"A\",\"correctOption\":\"a\",\"negativeMarks\":1,\"question_code\":\"CHE-SOM-4206\",\"question_type\":\"MCQ_SINGLE\",\"correct_option\":\"a\"},\"questionId\":\"9e3730a7-258a-4936-980f-1aa9cfb05f50\"},{\"id\":\"blk-1788170105297-1-14\",\"type\":\"question\",\"question\":{\"id\":\"910d1b87-31e9-4099-a460-8d1fc7d5cda4\",\"year\":null,\"marks\":4,\"source\":\"System Admin\",\"chapter\":\"Some Basic Concepts of Chemistry\",\"content\":[{\"id\":\"blk-1\",\"text\":\"<p>What mass of sodium chloride would be decomposed by 9.8 g of sulphuric acid if 12 g of sodium bisulphate and 2.75 g of hydrogen chloride were produced in a reaction?</p>\",\"type\":\"text\"}],\"options\":[{\"id\":\"2a89a68d-7994-4f2e-9d58-38f747569253\",\"key\":\"A\",\"content\":[{\"html\":\"<p>14.75 g</p>\",\"type\":\"text\"}],\"rawText\":\"<p>14.75 g</p>\",\"isCorrect\":false,\"option_key\":\"a\"},{\"id\":\"585e7df7-e82c-4ec1-a097-ddc911e12a28\",\"key\":\"B\",\"content\":[{\"html\":\"<p>3.8 g</p>\",\"type\":\"text\"}],\"rawText\":\"<p>3.8 g</p>\",\"isCorrect\":false,\"option_key\":\"b\"},{\"id\":\"d79c7715-c8df-49b1-9c5f-ff520990f870\",\"key\":\"C\",\"content\":[{\"html\":\"<p>4.95 g</p>\",\"type\":\"text\"}],\"rawText\":\"<p>4.95 g</p>\",\"isCorrect\":true,\"option_key\":\"c\"},{\"id