":\"B\",\"content\":[{\"html\":\"<p>73 g</p>\",\"type\":\"text\"}],\"rawText\":\"<p>73 g</p>\",\"isCorrect\":false,\"option_key\":\"b\"},{\"id\":\"8f7655a8-df71-4f12-aafd-919c22f8863e\",\"key\":\"C\",\"content\":[{\"html\":\"<p>50 g</p>\",\"type\":\"text\"}],\"rawText\":\"<p>50 g</p>\",\"isCorrect\":false,\"option_key\":\"c\"},{\"id\":\"1375de20-6a6d-4c3e-bcf6-db3a49a3e13f\",\"key\":\"D\",\"content\":[{\"html\":\"<p>100 g</p>\",\"type\":\"text\"}],\"rawText\":\"<p>100 g</p>\",\"isCorrect\":true,\"option_key\":\"d\"}],\"rawText\":\"<p>What mass of CaCO₃ decomposes to give 50 g of each CaO and CO₂?</p>\",\"subject\":\"Chemistry\",\"solution\":[],\"sectionId\":\"sec-2\",\"sectionName\":\"Part B: CHEMISTRY (MCQ)\",\"correctAnswer\":\"d\",\"correctOption\":\"d\",\"negativeMarks\":1,\"questionNumber\":117,\"explanationText\":[]}},{\"id\":\"blk-4cfb9f5d-fd5f-4d38-b646-c88bc4e9527d-0.7426619439912936\",\"type\":\"question\",\"question\":{\"id\":\"4cfb9f5d-fd5f-4d38-b646-c88bc4e9527d\",\"marks\":4,\"content\":[{\"id\":\"blk-1\",\"text\":\"<p><strong>The statements for laws of chemical combinations which are correctly given below. Mark the option which are correctly matched.</strong></p><p>I. Matter can neither be created nor destroyed: <strong>Law of conservation of mass</strong></p><p>II. A compound always contains exactly the same proportion of elements by weight: <strong>Law of definite proportions</strong></p><p>III. When gases combine they do so in a simple ratio by weight: <strong>Gay Lussac\'s Law</strong></p><p>IV. Equal volumes of gases at same temperature and pressure contain same number of molecules: <strong>Avogadro\'s Law</strong></p>\",\"type\":\"text\"}],\"options\":[{\"id\":\"69b9e0d4-8ed6-4357-bfdb-966b5aa70313\",\"key\":\"A\",\"content\":[{\"html\":\"<p>II and III only</p>\",\"type\":\"text\"}],\"rawText\":\"<p>II and III only</p>\",\"isCorrect\":false,\"option_key\":\"a\"},{\"id\":\"c88d7dc4-3080-4dce-b79e-6adc16485a40\",\"key\":\"B\",\"content\":[{\"html\":\"<p>I, III and IV only</p>\",\"type\":\"text\"}],\"rawText\":\"<p>I, III and IV only</p>\",\"isCorrect\":false,\"option_key\":\"b\"},{\"id\":\"02fa0a21-1549-4c2e-acbb-4431b6171c36\",\"key\":\"C\",\"content\":[{\"html\":\"<p>I, II and IV only</p>\",\"type\":\"text\"}],\"rawText\":\"<p>I, II and IV only</p>\",\"isCorrect\":true,\"option_key\":\"c\"},{\"id\":\"fb917b57-cfbd-4cdb-a866-e2b9f2c2b66a\",\"key\":\"D\",\"content\":[{\"html\":\"<p>II and IV only</p>\",\"type\":\"text\"}],\"rawText\":\"<p>II and IV only</p>\",\"isCorrect\":false,\"option_key\":\"d\"}],\"rawText\":\"<p><strong>The statements for laws of chemical combinations which are correctly given below. Mark the option which are correctly matched.</strong></p><p>I. Matter can neither be created nor destroyed: <strong>Law of conservation of mass</strong></p><p>II. A compound always contains exactly the same proportion of elements by weight: <strong>Law of definite proportions</strong></p><p>III. When gases combine they do so in a simple ratio by weight: <strong>Gay Lussac\'s Law</strong></p><p>IV. Equal volumes of gases at same temperature and pressure contain same number of molecules: <strong>Avogadro\'s Law</strong></p>\",\"subject\":\"Chemistry\",\"solution\":[],\"sectionId\":\"sec-2\",\"sectionName\":\"Part B: CHEMISTRY (MCQ)\",\"correctAnswer\":\"c\",\"correctOption\":\"c\",\"negativeMarks\":1,\"questionNumber\":118,\"explanationText\":[]}},{\"id\":\"blk-3bda23bf-9036-4946-b8be-7fe5b719474a-0.5689931645232679\",\"type\":\"question\",\"question\":{\"id\":\"3bda23bf-9036-4946-b8be-7fe5b719474a\",\"marks\":4,\"content\":[{\"id\":\"blk-1\",\"text\":\"<p><strong>Hydrogen combine with oxygen to give water vapour as follows:</strong></p><img src=\\\"/uploads/1788167836715_kbvr9i.png\\\" width=\\\"50%\\\" data-alignment=\\\"center\\\" style=\\\"width: 50%; max-width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;\\\"><p><strong>The above volume relationship is illustrated by which of the following law of chemical combination?</strong></p>\",\"type\":\"text\"}],\"options\":[{\"id\":\"06a63a27-478a-43aa-8c22-33588d95b3c1\",\"key\":\"A\",\"content\":[{\"html\":\"<p>Law of multiple proportions</p>\",\"type\":\"text\"}],\"rawText\":\"<p>Law of multiple proportions</p>\",\"isCorrect\":false,\"option_key\":\"a\"},{\"id\":\"c8597c5b-f3ae-47c9-a9da-8b57d6039d1c\",\"key\":\"B\",\"content\":[{\"html\":\"<p>Law of definite proportions</p>\",\"type\":\"text\"}],\"rawText\":\"<p>Law of definite proportions</p>\",\"isCorrect\":false,\"option_key\":\"b\"},{\"id\":\"d1b1ef93-e74d-47bd-ba6e-7e92a73cea0b\",\"key\":\"C\",\"content\":[{\"html\":\"<p>Gay Lussac\'s law of gaseous volumes</p>\",\"type\":\"text\"}],\"rawText\":\"<p>Gay Lussac\'s law of gaseous volumes</p>\",\"isCorrect\":true,\"option_key\":\"c\"},{\"id\":\"06ea43f5-ff7d-4813-9e66-db7882e0e654\",\"key\":\"D\",\"content\":[{\"html\":\"<p>Avogadro\'s law</p>\",\"type\":\"text\"}],\"rawText\":\"<p>Avogadro\'s law</p>\",\"isCorrect\":false,\"option_key\":\"d\"}],\"rawText\":\"<p><strong>Hydrogen combine with oxygen to give water vapour as follows:</strong></p><img src=\\\"/uploads/1788167836715_kbvr9i.png\\\" width=\\\"50%\\\" data-alignment=\\\"center\\\" style=\\\"width: 50%; max-width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;\\\"><p><strong>The above volume relationship is illustrated by which of the following law of chemical combination?</strong></p>\",\"subject\":\"Chemistry\",\"solution\":[],\"sectionId\":\"sec-2\",\"sectionName\":\"Part B: CHEMISTRY (MCQ)\",\"correctAnswer\":\"c\",\"correctOption\":\"c\",\"negativeMarks\":1,\"questionNumber\":119,\"explanationText\":[]}},{\"id\":\"blk-a0f3324f-8bc9-4637-88ed-a136efe21901-0.8919229571617894\",\"type\":\"question\",\"question\":{\"id\":\"a0f3324f-8bc9-4637-88ed-a136efe21901\",\"marks\":4,\"content\":[{\"id\":\"blk-1\",\"text\":\"<p>Which of the following statements illustrates the law of multiple proportions?</p>\",\"type\":\"text\"}],\"options\":[{\"id\":\"fc1d42d9-82a5-4c83-a9ce-3b3c6caa9c13\",\"key\":\"A\",\"content\":[{\"html\":\"<p>An element forms two oxides, XO and XO₂ containing 50% and 60% oxygen respectively. The ratio of masses of oxygen which combines with 1 g of element is 2 : 3.</p>\",\"type\":\"text\"}],\"rawText\":\"<p>An element forms two oxides, XO and XO₂ containing 50% and 60% oxygen respectively. The ratio of masses of oxygen which combines with 1 g of element is 2 : 3.</p>\",\"isCorrect\":true,\"option_key\":\"a\"},{\"id\":\"ceeda6a3-5dd0-4fba-8351-cdf063f6d5f7\",\"key\":\"B\",\"content\":[{\"html\":\"<p>One volume of nitrogen always combines with three volumes of hydrogen to form two volumes of ammonia.</p>\",\"type\":\"text\"}],\"rawText\":\"<p>One volume of nitrogen always combines with three volumes of hydrogen to form two volumes of ammonia.</p>\",\"isCorrect\":false,\"option_key\":\"b\"},{\"id\":\"ab0b7872-030a-4c40-ae6e-cada5a08ea57\",\"key\":\"C\",\"content\":[{\"html\":\"<p>3.47 g of BaCl₂ reacts with 2.36 g of Na₂SO₄ to give 3.88 g of BaSO₄ and 1.95 g of NaCl</p>\",\"type\":\"text\"}],\"rawText\":\"<p>3.47 g of BaCl₂ reacts with 2.36 g of Na₂SO₄ to give 3.88 g of BaSO₄ and 1.95 g of NaCl</p>\",\"isCorrect\":false,\"option_key\":\"c\"},{\"id\":\"0186e137-9219-4e17-b2a6-472886572dad\",\"key\":\"D\",\"content\":[{\"html\":\"<p>20 volumes of ammonia gives 10 volumes of N₂ and 30 volumes of H₂ at constant temperature and pressure.</p>\",\"type\":\"text\"}],\"rawText\":\"<p>20 volumes of ammonia gives 10 volumes of N₂ and 30 volumes of H₂ at constant temperature and pressure.</p>\",\"isCorrect\":false,\"option_key\":\"d\"}],\"rawText\":\"<p>Which of the following statements illustrates the law of multiple proportions?</p>\",\"subject\":\"Chemistry\",\"solution\":[],\"sectionId\":\"sec-2\",\"sectionName\":\"Part B: CHEMISTRY (MCQ)\",\"correctAnswer\":\"a\",\"correctOption\":\"a\",\"negativeMarks\":1,\"questionNumber\":120,\"explanationText\":[]}},{\"id\":\"blk-3a3de7a5-272c-47e5-98d8-679e2658bf70-0.929934790650168\",\"type\":\"question\",\"question\":{\"id\":\"3a3de7a5-272c-47e5-98d8-679e2658bf70\",\"marks\":4,\"content\":[{\"id\":\"blk-1\",\"text\":\"<p>Which of the following pairs illustrates the law of multiple proportions?</p>\",\"type\":\"text\"}],\"options\":[{\"id\":\"f2113083-7bc9-43cf-b3e8-1774a71d0dbf\",\"key\":\"A\",\"content\":[{\"html\":\"<p>PH₃, HCl</p>\",\"type\":\"text\"}],\"rawText\":\"<p>PH₃, HCl</p>\",\"isCorrect\":false,\"option_key\":\"a\"},{\"id\":\"41f3d455-6f9d-475f-ada6-3ce21a038ae3\",\"key\":\"B\",\"content\":[{\"html\":\"<p>PbO, PbO₂</p>\",\"type\":\"text\"}],\"rawText\":\"<p>PbO, PbO₂</p>\",\"isCorrect\":true,\"option_key\":\"b\"},{\"id\":\"0412020b-52a0-4100-a57d-5fbd3929ad74\",\"key\":\"C\",\"content\":[{\"html\":\"<p>H₂S, SO₂</p>\",\"type\":\"text\"}],\"rawText\":\"<p>H₂S, SO₂</p>\",\"isCorrect\":false,\"option_key\":\"c\"},{\"id\":\"17a0daee-c872-4904-95e9-23b1283d9aad\",\"key\":\"D\",\"content\":[{\"html\":\"<p>CuCl₂, CuSO₄</p>\",\"type\":\"text\"}],\"rawText\":\"<p>CuCl₂, CuSO₄</p>\",\"isCorrect\":false,\"option_key\":\"d\"}],\"rawText\":\"<p>Which of the following pairs illustrates the law of multiple proportions?</p>\",\"subject\":\"Chemistry\",\"solution\":[],\"sectionId\":\"sec-2\",\"sectionName\":\"Part B: CHEMISTRY (MCQ)\",\"correctAnswer\":\"b\",\"correctOption\":\"b\",\"negativeMarks\":1,\"questionNumber\":121,\"explanationText\":[]}},{\"id\":\"blk-02cad7bc-8bc8-470f-93ee-5ab9bc7fd21b-0.0002887541881487765\",\"type\":\"question\",\"question\":{\"id\":\"02cad7bc-8bc8-470f-93ee-5ab9bc7fd21b\",\"marks\":4,\"content\":[{\"id\":\"blk-1\",\"text\":\"<p>Which of the following statements about Avogadro\'s hypothesis is correct?</p>\",\"type\":\"text\"}],\"options\":[{\"id\":\"8a7af629-3f08-46d2-939f-50da44ed77b4\",\"key\":\"A\",\"content\":[{\"html\":\"<p>Under similar conditions of temperature and pressure, gases react with each other in simple ratio.</p>\",\"type\":\"text\"}],\