":\"d1b16e39-9fd2-4d0f-820d-a27b47bd9bbd\",\"key\":\"D\",\"content\":[{\"html\":\"<p>sp³, sp², sp</p>\",\"type\":\"text\"}],\"rawText\":\"<p>sp³, sp², sp</p>\",\"isCorrect\":false,\"option_key\":\"d\"}],\"rawText\":\"<p data-align=\\\"justify\\\" style=\\\"text-align: justify;\\\">Which type of hybridisation is shown by carbon atoms from left to right in the given compound</p><p>CH₂ = CH — C ≡ N?</p>\",\"subject\":\"Chemistry\",\"solution\":[],\"sectionId\":\"sec-2\",\"sectionName\":\"Part B: CHEMISTRY (MCQ)\",\"correctAnswer\":\"a\",\"correctOption\":\"a\",\"negativeMarks\":1,\"questionNumber\":125,\"explanationText\":[]}},{\"id\":\"blk-65042ed3-9609-49d6-bfbf-e0be5b875248-0.8321663904284294\",\"type\":\"question\",\"question\":{\"id\":\"65042ed3-9609-49d6-bfbf-e0be5b875248\",\"marks\":4,\"content\":[{\"id\":\"blk-1\",\"text\":\"<p><strong>The main drawback of Dalton\'s atomic theory is</strong></p><p>I. It could not explain the law of gaseous volumes.<br>II. It could not explain why atoms of different elements have different masses, sizes, etc.<br>III. It could not explain how and why atoms combine to form molecules.</p><p></p>\",\"type\":\"text\"}],\"options\":[{\"id\":\"6e53c347-6acc-435e-ac03-6526bb54e7aa\",\"key\":\"A\",\"content\":[{\"html\":\"<p>I and III only</p>\",\"type\":\"text\"}],\"rawText\":\"<p>I and III only</p>\",\"isCorrect\":false,\"option_key\":\"a\"},{\"id\":\"f3d3711d-1700-4244-b262-c3354a0a0ae7\",\"key\":\"B\",\"content\":[{\"html\":\"<p>II and III only</p>\",\"type\":\"text\"}],\"rawText\":\"<p>II and III only</p>\",\"isCorrect\":false,\"option_key\":\"b\"},{\"id\":\"a7c37b50-be1a-41d0-9ccf-06bdb6cdded2\",\"key\":\"C\",\"content\":[{\"html\":\"<p>II only</p>\",\"type\":\"text\"}],\"rawText\":\"<p>II only</p>\",\"isCorrect\":false,\"option_key\":\"c\"},{\"id\":\"4436dab7-7207-4586-9f94-9e22d833b33c\",\"key\":\"D\",\"content\":[{\"html\":\"<p>I, II and III</p>\",\"type\":\"text\"}],\"rawText\":\"<p>I, II and III</p>\",\"isCorrect\":true,\"option_key\":\"d\"}],\"rawText\":\"<p><strong>The main drawback of Dalton\'s atomic theory is</strong></p><p>I. It could not explain the law of gaseous volumes.<br>II. It could not explain why atoms of different elements have different masses, sizes, etc.<br>III. It could not explain how and why atoms combine to form molecules.</p><p></p>\",\"subject\":\"Chemistry\",\"solution\":[],\"sectionId\":\"sec-2\",\"sectionName\":\"Part B: CHEMISTRY (MCQ)\",\"correctAnswer\":\"d\",\"correctOption\":\"d\",\"negativeMarks\":1,\"questionNumber\":126,\"explanationText\":[]}},{\"id\":\"blk-d060d925-6887-4f96-9e42-7b2c50e1dc70-0.31522005446967016\",\"type\":\"question\",\"question\":{\"id\":\"d060d925-6887-4f96-9e42-7b2c50e1dc70\",\"marks\":4,\"content\":[{\"id\":\"blk-1\",\"text\":\"<p>Choose the correct statement about I, II, III.</p><p></p><img src=\\\"/uploads/1788158198119_c99ack.png\\\" width=\\\"50%\\\" data-alignment=\\\"center\\\" style=\\\"width: 50%; max-width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;\\\"><p></p>\",\"type\":\"text\"}],\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a57e951a-31aa-4afd-b2f5-47da4f02c4ae\",\"key\":\"A\",\"content\":[{\"html\":\"<p>I and II have definite volume but III does not have this property.</p>\",\"type\":\"text\"}],\"rawText\":\"<p>I and II have definite volume but III does not have this property.</p>\",\"isCorrect\":false,\"option_key\":\"a\"},{\"id\":\"7f755b4a-75a4-4e6e-84ed-926fa5481b94\",\"key\":\"B\",\"content\":[{\"html\":\"<p>I, II and III are interconvertible by changing the conditions of temperature and pressure.</p>\",\"type\":\"text\"}],\"rawText\":\"<p>I, II and III are interconvertible by changing the conditions of temperature and pressure.</p>\",\"isCorrect\":false,\"option_key\":\"b\"},{\"id\":\"5213e3a7-99a1-42bd-84a7-535c6a7bbe63\",\"key\":\"C\",\"content\":[{\"html\":\"<p>In the particles of I, freedom of movement is large.</p>\",\"type\":\"text\"}],\"rawText\":\"<p>In the particles of I, freedom of movement is large.</p>\",\"isCorrect\":false,\"option_key\":\"c\"},{\"id\":\"3a3c3867-934a-4cb8-936a-c6badebbe9dd\",\"key\":\"D\",\"content\":[{\"html\":\"<p>Both (a) and (b).</p>\",\"type\":\"text\"}],\"rawText\":\"<p>Both (a) and (b).</p>\",\"isCorrect\":true,\"option_key\":\"d\"}],\"rawText\":\"<p>Choose the correct statement about I, II, III.</p><p></p><img src=\\\"/uploads/1788158198119_c99ack.png\\\" width=\\\"50%\\\" data-alignment=\\\"center\\\" style=\\\"width: 50%; max-width: 100%; height: auto; display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;\\\"><p></p>\",\"subject\":\"Chemistry\",\"solution\":\"<p>Both (a) and (b).</p>\",\"sectionId\":\"sec-2\",\"sectionName\":\"Part B: CHEMISTRY (MCQ)\",\"correctAnswer\":\"d\",\"correctOption\":\"d\",\"negativeMarks\":1,\"questionNumber\":127,\"explanationText\":\"<p>Both (a) and (b).</p>\"}},{\"id\":\"blk-205085c0-80dd-471b-9974-ee62626e00fe-0.46687483120234674\",\"type\":\"question\",\"question\":{\"id\":\"205085c0-80dd-471b-9974-ee62626e00fe\",\"marks\":4,\"content\":[{\"id\":\"blk-1\",\"text\":\"<p>Oxygen occurs in nature as a mixture of isotopes ¹⁶O, ¹⁷O and ¹⁸O having atomic masses of 15.995 u, 16.999 u and 17.999 u and relative abundance of 99.763%, 0.037% and 0.200% respectively. What is the average atomic mass of oxygen?</p>\",\"type\":\"text\"}],\"options\":[{\"id\":\"47329889-3b79-479a-b17a-4272180844a6\",\"key\":\"A\",\"content\":[{\"html\":\"<p>15.999 u</p>\",\"type\":\"text\"}],\"rawText\":\"<p>15.999 u</p>\",\"isCorrect\":true,\"option_key\":\"a\"},{\"id\":\"43894888-c1da-46ab-96fe-36ae1d078619\",\"key\":\"B\",\"content\":[{\"html\":\"<p>16.999 u</p>\",\"type\":\"text\"}],\"rawText\":\"<p>16.999 u</p>\",\"isCorrect\":false,\"option_key\":\"b\"},{\"id\":\"8460140c-3d58-4ebd-917b-83e1ebab39c6\",\"key\":\"C\",\"content\":[{\"html\":\"<p>17.999 u</p>\",\"type\":\"text\"}],\"rawText\":\"<p>17.999 u</p>\",\"isCorrect\":false,\"option_key\":\"c\"},{\"id\":\"262b9916-d8a5-4575-9db6-8e8431b31d00\",\"key\":\"D\",\"co